            <ArticleTitle>Plasma separation using a membrane.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>649-653</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1473-0502(17)30159-3</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.transci.2017.08.008</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Plasma separation using a membrane is clinically used as plasmapheresis therapy, such as plasma exchange (PE), double-filtration plasmapheresis (DFPP), or plasma adsorption (PA) modalities. Plasma separation is performed either by centrifugation or by filtration, which involves a permeable hollow fiber membrane. A plasma separator with a hollow fiber membrane was first developed in Japan in the 1980s. It has been used for more than 30 years with continuous technical modifications. Treatment with a membrane-type plasma separator is a safe and well-established modality for many drug-resistant and/or refractory diseases. Currently, national health insurance covers plasmapheresis for ∼30 diseases in Japan. Membrane-type plasma separators are used not only in Japan but also in many other countries. Plasmapheresis treatments with a membrane-type plasma separator are an important alternative for patients with drug-resistant and/or refractory diseases at present and in the near future.</AbstractText>
